report **STATUS:** approved  
  view show view1 V1 Columbia publication output by research field, 2021–2024
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── answer ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Columbia’s publication output fell 1.6% from 2021 to 2024, even though 13 research fields gained output and 13 lost output. The total rebounded in 2024 after   
two years of decline: 14,175 → 13,636 → 13,616 → 13,944 works. [T2]                                                                                             

                                                         
 Largest changes by work count        2021  2024  Change 
 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 Computer Science                      801   979    +178 
 Physics and Astronomy                 567   668    +101 
 Business, Management and Accounting   154   225     +71 
 Arts and Humanities                   474   389     −85 
 Environmental Science                 769   681     −88 
 Earth and Planetary Sciences          667   456    −211 
                                                         

Business, Management and Accounting was the only field to grow every year. Earth and Planetary Sciences, Environmental Science, and Chemistry declined every    
year. Medicine remained the largest field in all four years, despite finishing 80 works below its 2021 count. [T1]                                              

Explore every field in the interactive view. The bars show the change from 2021 to 2024, and the four year columns give exact counts. Use More fields to see the
rest of the ranking, filter by OpenAlex domain, or switch from work counts to percentage change. Percentages can be large when the starting count is small:     
Veterinary went from 1 to 6 works, a 500% increase. [V1, T1]                                                                                                    

Each work is counted once by publication year and assigned to the OpenAlex field of its primary topic; unclassified works are shown separately. The counts      
include all work types in the fixed OpenAlex snapshot, not a complete Columbia publication census. [T1, T3]                                                     

Two useful follow-ups: Which fields gained the most share of Columbia’s output? And do the same trends hold if we count only journal articles?                  
